1. The Narrative Conductor

Let the complete world/story state be:

W_t

The audience never receives W_t directly.

The narrator selects a projection:

R_t = \Pi_t(W_{0:t})

The audience reconstructs:

M_{t+1}=U(M_t,R_t)

So narrative is not merely:

W_0\rightarrow W_1\rightarrow W_2

It is:

W
\xrightarrow{\text{Conductor}}
R_1,R_2,R_3,\ldots
\xrightarrow{\text{Audience}}
M_1,M_2,M_3,\ldots

The reveal is when new information changes not just the future but the interpretation of the past:

R_t \Rightarrow
M_{0:t-1}' \neq M_{0:t-1}

That is huge.

Ordinary information says:

Now you know something new.

A good reveal says:

Now everything you already knew means something different.

That’s a Pantheon-grade transformation.

The Conductor’s optimization problem

\Pi^*=
\arg\max_{\Pi}
\sum_t
\left[
S_t
R_t
C_t
A_t
\right]
-\lambda Q_t

where:

* S = surprise
* R = relevance
* C = coherence
* A = affect/attention
* Q = confusion/noise

Too little surprise = boring.

Too much = nonsense.

Too obvious = exposition.

Too hidden = nobody reconstructs it.

The conductor rides the ridge.

Tightrope.

And repeated successful routes become—

trope.

