2. The invariants underneath essentially all tropes

I think the deepest common structure is even more compact than my previous answer:

\boxed{\text{Identity through difference}}

Something must remain invariant while something else changes.

Otherwise there is neither story nor recognition.

Underneath that sit about ten primitive invariants:

\mathcal N=
\{I,\Delta,G,C,K,P,\tau,R,V,\Omega\}

Where:

I Identity — what remains the same?

\Delta Difference — what changed?

G Goal — toward what?

C Constraint — what prevents direct traversal?

K Knowledge — who knows what?

P Perspective — who sees what?

\tau Time — when is it known/acted upon?

R Relation — who/what is connected?

V Value/cost — what is gained or sacrificed?

\Omega Recurrence — what repeats?

Every trope is a composition over these.

That’s why tropes cross cultures.

The costume varies.

The causal geometry survives translation.

