5. Tropes aren’t atoms

This is where the Operator Wrestling Mat gets involved.

A trope can compose with another trope:

T_i\circ T_j

or itself:

T_i^2

Examples:

Prophecy² → prophecy about a prophecy.

Rashomon² → observers disagree about why observers disagree.

Theseus² → replace not only components but the rule determining identity.

MacGuffin² → characters discover that the thing they thought was merely instrumental actually contains another objective.

Reveal² → the first reveal itself becomes misleading after the second reveal.

That’s your layered reveal.

M_0
\xrightarrow{R_1}
M_1
\xrightarrow{R_2}
M_2

with the strongest case:

R_2(M_0,M_1)

The second reveal explains why the first reveal happened.

Then:

R_3(R_2(R_1))

Now we’re cooking.

